1
00:00:00,208 --> 00:00:03,606
So how can you access locally running large

2
00:00:03,718 --> 00:00:06,459
language models programmatically with help

3
00:00:06,587 --> 00:00:07,645
of LM Studio?

4
00:00:08,927 --> 00:00:12,454
Well, as a first step in LM Studio, you

5
00:00:12,454 --> 00:00:15,467
should go to power user or developer since

6
00:00:15,659 --> 00:00:17,518
now it is all about development.

7
00:00:18,528 --> 00:00:21,381
And then there to this developer view, this

8
00:00:21,429 --> 00:00:22,728
developer area here.

9
00:00:24,218 --> 00:00:27,648
And there you must switch this status of the

10
00:00:27,648 --> 00:00:30,730
LM Studio LM Studio server, which is the

11
00:00:30,827 --> 00:00:32,428
thing you will be able to access

12
00:00:32,444 --> 00:00:34,804
programmatically, from stopped

13
00:00:35,418 --> 00:00:35,887
to running.

14
00:00:37,439 --> 00:00:40,188
As a result of doing that, you'll get a log

15
00:00:40,285 --> 00:00:42,160
output down there which shows you log

16
00:00:42,289 --> 00:00:45,054
messages from requests that were sent to

17
00:00:45,054 --> 00:00:45,668
that server.

18
00:00:46,331 --> 00:00:48,094
You can clear that and you'll see any new

19
00:00:48,127 --> 00:00:48,661
messages

20
00:00:49,325 --> 00:00:52,353
as they arrive or as new requests hit the

21
00:00:52,353 --> 00:00:52,628
server.

22
00:00:53,932 --> 00:00:57,442
You see the API endpoints, so the URLs you

23
00:00:57,474 --> 00:00:59,825
could say, to which you can send requests

24
00:00:59,874 --> 00:01:00,018
to,

25
00:01:00,657 --> 00:01:01,274
for example,

26
00:01:01,875 --> 00:01:04,716
get a list of available models or to

27
00:01:04,716 --> 00:01:07,135
generate a new response from a model.

28
00:01:09,018 --> 00:01:12,096
And you see the domain to which you have to

29
00:01:12,096 --> 00:01:14,352
send these requests. So you send them to a

30
00:01:14,352 --> 00:01:17,027
combination of that domain and then this

31
00:01:17,174 --> 00:01:17,616
endpoint.

32
00:01:18,484 --> 00:01:20,663
Now, if you don't know anything about

33
00:01:20,843 --> 00:01:22,010
writing code, then

34
00:01:22,684 --> 00:01:25,508
this lecture and this part of this section

35
00:01:25,637 --> 00:01:27,418
won't be for you because, of course,

36
00:01:27,498 --> 00:01:30,114
programmatically using large language models

37
00:01:30,772 --> 00:01:34,318
only is interesting if you know how to write

38
00:01:34,318 --> 00:01:36,484
code or if you at least have a basic

39
00:01:36,484 --> 00:01:38,714
understanding of how programming works.

40
00:01:39,308 --> 00:01:41,073
Of course, you can use a large language

41
00:01:41,202 --> 00:01:43,159
model to generate code for you, but you

42
00:01:43,159 --> 00:01:45,438
still need to have a general understanding

43
00:01:45,791 --> 00:01:48,823
of what an API is and what it means to send

44
00:01:48,823 --> 00:01:50,139
a request to an API.

45
00:01:51,247 --> 00:01:53,991
But if you got that, then LM Studio has you

46
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:56,752
covered because now we got a running server

47
00:01:57,089 --> 00:01:59,352
running locally on our system, only

48
00:01:59,448 --> 00:02:03,172
accessible by us here on our machine. So not

49
00:02:03,268 --> 00:02:05,579
available to the World Wide Web unless you

50
00:02:05,596 --> 00:02:08,067
configured your machine to be reachable from

51
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:10,427
everyone else out there, of course, which by

52
00:02:10,427 --> 00:02:11,518
default won't be the case.

53
00:02:12,417 --> 00:02:14,359
And you then also get some settings you can

54
00:02:14,359 --> 00:02:16,684
tweak here. About that server. Now, for

55
00:02:16,684 --> 00:02:17,646
example, the port,

56
00:02:18,287 --> 00:02:20,115
which is also reflected here, to which the

57
00:02:20,115 --> 00:02:21,413
requests have to be sent.

58
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:24,844
You can configure just-in-time model

59
00:02:24,844 --> 00:02:25,630
loading, which means

60
00:02:26,207 --> 00:02:28,836
if a request would be sent for a model

61
00:02:28,868 --> 00:02:31,802
that's not currently loaded in LM Studio, it

62
00:02:31,898 --> 00:02:33,934
would then be loaded on demand.

63
00:02:34,639 --> 00:02:36,258
You can turn this off if you don't want

64
00:02:36,258 --> 00:02:38,551
this, but if you turn it on, the advantage

65
00:02:38,551 --> 00:02:40,490
is that you don't have to load models in

66
00:02:40,490 --> 00:02:43,667
advance. Of course, requests for models that

67
00:02:43,843 --> 00:02:45,705
aren't loaded yet will take a bit longer

68
00:02:45,705 --> 00:02:47,710
because the model has to be loaded first.

69
00:02:49,139 --> 00:02:50,839
And you can also control that a model should

70
00:02:50,839 --> 00:02:53,760
be auto-unloaded if it hasn't been used for

71
00:02:54,129 --> 00:02:56,536
a while, for 60 minutes, for example, as a

72
00:02:56,536 --> 00:02:57,033
default.

73
00:02:58,477 --> 00:03:00,965
So these are some settings you can tweak,

74
00:03:01,029 --> 00:03:03,404
but with that, the server is up and running.

75
00:03:03,917 --> 00:03:05,409
And the question then, of course, is

76
00:03:06,147 --> 00:03:08,731
how can you use that server?

77
00:03:09,295 --> 00:03:10,006
And the answer,

78
00:03:10,571 --> 00:03:13,220
as you also find in the official docs, is,

79
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:14,335
for example,

80
00:03:14,949 --> 00:03:16,823
by using the OpenAI

81
00:03:17,840 --> 00:03:18,244
SDK,

82
00:03:19,853 --> 00:03:20,451
this one here.

83
00:03:21,468 --> 00:03:23,777
And that might be a bit weird because the

84
00:03:23,794 --> 00:03:26,733
idea behind this SDK, as the name kind of

85
00:03:26,733 --> 00:03:27,217
implies,

86
00:03:27,776 --> 00:03:30,735
is to use it in conjunction with the OpenAI

87
00:03:31,009 --> 00:03:34,808
API, so the proprietary models provided by

88
00:03:34,889 --> 00:03:35,439
OpenAI.

89
00:03:36,032 --> 00:03:37,107
Which is why

90
00:03:37,860 --> 00:03:40,811
when visiting the OpenAI documentation, you

91
00:03:40,923 --> 00:03:44,099
find comparable instructions about using the

92
00:03:44,131 --> 00:03:45,253
OpenAI SDK,

93
00:03:46,119 --> 00:03:48,445
just that here it of course makes sense.

94
00:03:49,166 --> 00:03:52,598
But indeed, this SDK, this OpenAI SDK, has

95
00:03:52,711 --> 00:03:55,245
become pretty much the de facto standard for

96
00:03:55,373 --> 00:03:56,127
interacting

97
00:03:56,720 --> 00:04:00,857
with LLM APIs. Many other providers also

98
00:04:00,874 --> 00:04:03,936
adopted it. For example, Google also allows

99
00:04:03,936 --> 00:04:06,597
you to interact with Gemini, which is their

100
00:04:06,741 --> 00:04:09,097
paid models, not the free ones, but Gemini

101
00:04:09,306 --> 00:04:10,123
is the paid

102
00:04:10,893 --> 00:04:12,079
LLM provided by Google

103
00:04:12,736 --> 00:04:14,435
through the OpenAI SDK.

104
00:04:15,782 --> 00:04:18,796
And it's the same for LM Studio. The API

105
00:04:19,164 --> 00:04:22,146
that is provided by that LM Studio server

106
00:04:22,370 --> 00:04:25,544
that's spun up by setting it to running.

107
00:04:26,394 --> 00:04:28,654
So the API provided by that server here

108
00:04:29,408 --> 00:04:32,133
is essentially in line with the structure of

109
00:04:32,133 --> 00:04:33,575
the OpenAI API.

110
00:04:34,297 --> 00:04:37,775
And can therefore be used through the OpenAI

111
00:04:37,839 --> 00:04:38,240
SDK.

112
00:04:38,785 --> 00:04:40,965
The only thing you need to do when aiming to

113
00:04:40,965 --> 00:04:41,430
do so

114
00:04:42,071 --> 00:04:44,973
is when initializing that OpenAI SDK,

115
00:04:45,534 --> 00:04:48,211
you have to set the base URL parameter here

116
00:04:48,804 --> 00:04:52,026
to this value, which is the domain or the

117
00:04:52,170 --> 00:04:55,328
URL of this locally running LM Studio

118
00:04:55,857 --> 00:04:56,194
API.

119
00:04:57,540 --> 00:05:00,089
Because otherwise, without setting this, it

120
00:05:00,121 --> 00:05:02,478
would try to send requests to the actual

121
00:05:02,590 --> 00:05:05,315
OpenAI API, which is of course not what we

122
00:05:05,331 --> 00:05:05,475
want.

123
00:05:06,533 --> 00:05:08,616
Instead, we wanna send requests to our

124
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:10,203
locally running model,

125
00:05:10,940 --> 00:05:13,135
and that can be done through that base URL.

126
00:05:14,609 --> 00:05:17,349
Now, of course, it's also worth pointing out

127
00:05:17,349 --> 00:05:19,897
that you could also communicate with that

128
00:05:20,138 --> 00:05:23,599
OpenAI-compatible API that is exposed by LM

129
00:05:23,599 --> 00:05:24,064
Studio

130
00:05:24,625 --> 00:05:28,214
without using the OpenAI SDK. You can just

131
00:05:28,246 --> 00:05:32,060
send a regular HTTP request with help of any

132
00:05:32,172 --> 00:05:35,121
programming language you want. It is just an

133
00:05:35,121 --> 00:05:37,590
API endpoint you have to send a request to

134
00:05:37,766 --> 00:05:38,215
after all.

135
00:05:39,385 --> 00:05:41,998
But it will be an API endpoint that's in

136
00:05:42,014 --> 00:05:44,915
line with the OpenAI API and therefore you

137
00:05:44,915 --> 00:05:47,448
can use many of the features provided by the

138
00:05:47,464 --> 00:05:48,602
OpenAI API

139
00:05:49,147 --> 00:05:51,839
just here with our locally running API

140
00:05:52,032 --> 00:05:54,148
that's provided by LM Studio. So that's

141
00:05:54,212 --> 00:05:54,949
quite convenient.

142
00:05:55,734 --> 00:05:57,465
And therefore we can now take a look at some

143
00:05:57,658 --> 00:05:58,427
code examples.
